            <ArticleTitle>[Progressive dyspnea, angina pectoris and weight loss in a 76-year old patient].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>400-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Cardiac metastases are a rare finding. The primary diagnosis of a malignant disease by cardial metastases is a curiosity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE REPORT" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 76-year-old patient presented with dyspnea, angina pectoris and body weight loss. Echocardiography showed a circular pericardial effusion and intramyocardial masses with high echodensity in the right atrial and the right ventricular wall. MIR was not diagnostic of the intramyocardial masses. The pericardiocentesis was negative. The procedures revealed an intestinal type gastric adenocarcinoma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Echocardiography is an established method for the diagnosis of cardiac metastases.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
